{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"REOPENED WORK — two defects found post-merge, both fixed in one change.\n\nDEFECT 1 (live on main): `scripts/dependency_audit.py` still emitted the retired flag in the dependency-advisory canary's auto-close argv. It landed via 0767c38ebb (ticket 63e8) which merged AFTER the rename — two concurrent agents crossed, neither could see the other.\n\nRUNTIME IMPACT — verified, not assumed. The parser's explicit rejecting branch fires: CommandError, returncode 1, message 'Error: --force-close was renamed to --force (ticket 24f7) and no longer exists.' `cmd_advisory_alert` does `print(stderr); return rc`, so the canary's auto-close path exits non-zero and the scheduled job goes red. Crucially it is NOT a silent no-op — the rejecting branch this ticket added is what turns it into a loud failure. Fixed to `--force={reason}`, matching the already-migrated `scripts/canary_bridge.py`. No other stale assumption in the surrounding code: `--reason` and `--force=` are independent slots under the new contract and were already passed separately.\n\nDEFECT 2 (why defect 1 survived): the tree-wide acceptance criterion was verified by a one-time manual `git grep` that passed at that moment. Nothing re-checked it, so a file landing minutes later was uncaught. Now committed as a real test.\n\nThe exemption set in the AC was VERIFIED rather than trusted, and was wrong in one place: `src/rebar/_commands/transition.py` does NOT need exemption — it assembles the literal from parts (`_RETIRED_FORCE_CLOSE = \"--force\" + \"-close\"`), so it never carries the string. The earned set is 6 files: the three frozen corpora, CHANGELOG.md, docs/release-notes.md, and this ticket's own test file (its module docstring prose). A second test asserts every exemption is STILL earned, so a stale entry cannot silently widen the blind spot.\n\nGuard derives its file list from `git ls-files` (tracked files), not a directory denylist — the d5ae lesson. Exemptions are exact paths, never directory prefixes, so even a new frozen corpus in the same directory must be consciously weighed.\n\nAlso strengthened the call site itself: `test_advisory_alert_close_argv_is_accepted_by_the_real_transition_parser` feeds the emitted argv into the live `_parse_flags`. That is stronger than grep — a future rename fails at the call site whatever it is renamed to. The pre-existing close test asserted only positional slots, which is precisely how the flag escaped the suite.\n\nRED PROVEN BEFORE GREEN for both new tests:\n- tree-wide guard: FAILED with exactly one offender, ['scripts/dependency_audit.py'];\n- call-site parser test: FAILED with the CommandError above.\nBoth green after the fix; `make lint` and `make typecheck` clean."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786109413528955001,"uuid":"8c0e7a2f-303c-4f9e-872a-acfa3132e3b5"}